cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The signaling cost of finite-speed hidden influences"
message: >-
  This is an unrefereed research memorandum. It was produced by AI systems under
  human direction and no claim in it has been verified by a human domain expert.
  Please cite it as a preprint-stage memorandum, not as a reviewed result.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Steven
    name-particle: W.
    family-names: Jones
    email: stevenwarejones@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  Manuscript, exact-arithmetic certificates and independent verifiers for the
  forced-signaling quantity Sigma_HIC: the minimum operational signaling that any
  conditionally-local finite-speed hidden-influence model must exhibit while
  reproducing the observable marginals of a quantum behavior in a blind-pair
  scenario. For the four-qubit linear-cluster correlations of arXiv:2608.05271,
  Sigma_HIC = (sqrt(2)-1)/4 exactly, with primal and dual certificates over
  Q(sqrt(2)); the measurable tradeoff S4 <= 6 + 8*Delta_sig carries an exact
  rational certificate with a provably optimal constant.
keywords:
  - quantum nonlocality
  - Bell inequalities
  - hidden influences
  - finite-speed causal models
  - superluminal signaling
  - linear programming certificates
  - cluster states
license: MIT
